6. Applications of Stack

Function calls (Call Stack)
Expression evaluation
syntax parsing
Undo and Redo operations
Backtracking algorithms
Depth First Search (DFS)
Reversal of data

7. Expression Handling Using Stack

Infix to Postfix conversion
Infix to Prefix conversion
Postfix expression evaluation
Parenthesis matching

8. Stack in Memory Management

Each function call creates a stack frame that stores:
Local variables
Function parameters
Return address
Excessive recursion can cause stack overflow.

9. Advantages and Limitations of Stack

Advantages

Simple and fast operations
Efficient memory usage
Useful for recursive problems

Limitations

No random access
Limited access to elements
Fixed size in array implementation

PART 2: QUEUE
1. Definition of Queue

A queue is a linear data structure that follows:

FIFO (First In, First Out)
the element inserted first is removed first.

2. Queue Terminology

Front: Position from which elements are removed
Rear: Position where elements are inserted
Overflow: Queue is full
Underflow: Queue is empty

3. Queue Operations

Enqueue: Insert element at the rear
Dequeue: Remove element from the front
Front / Rear
isEmpty / isFull

Time Complexity for all operations: O(1)

4. Queue Implementation

Array implementation
Linked list implementation
Circular array implementation

5. Types of Queue

Simple Queue
Circular Queue
Priority Queue
Deque (Double Ended Queue)

6. Applications of Queue

CPU scheduling
Disk scheduling
Printer management
Breadth First Search (BFS)
Sliding window problems
Order processing systems

7. Queue in Operating Systems

